{"name":"Light Skyway","hex":"#c2e3e8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"},{"term":"skyway","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An aeroplane route.","An elevated road."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/skyway"}],"entries":[{"title":"Skyway","blocks":[{"text":"A skyway, skybridge, skywalk or sky walkway is an elevated pedestrian walkway connecting two or more buildings in an urban area, or connecting elevated points within mountainous recreational zones. Urban skyways very often take the form of enclosed or covered footbridges that protect pedestrians from the weather. Open-top modern skyways in mountains now often have glass bottoms. Sometimes enclosed urban skywalks are made almost totally from glass, including ceilings, walls and floors. Some urban skyways function strictly as linear parks designed for walking."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/2008-03-14_Spring_St_skyways_in_Atlanta.jpg/330px-2008-03-14_Spring_St_skyways_in_Atlanta.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skyway","wikidata":"Q1361100"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light","wikidata":"Q9128"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Pottery_tower_6.JPG/500px-Pottery_tower_6.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Pottery_tower_6.JPG/1280px-Pottery_tower_6.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Pottery_tower_6.JPG/500px-Pottery_tower_6.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Han dynasty (202 BC – 220 AD) Chinese miniature model of two residential towers joined by a skyway","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APottery_tower_6.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Antonio_Contin_-_Ponte_dei_sospiri_%28Venice%29.jpg/500px-Antonio_Contin_-_Ponte_dei_sospiri_%28Venice%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Antonio_Contin_-_Ponte_dei_sospiri_%28Venice%29.jpg/1280px-Antonio_Contin_-_Ponte_dei_sospiri_%28Venice%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Antonio_Contin_-_Ponte_dei_sospiri_%28Venice%29.jpg/500px-Antonio_Contin_-_Ponte_dei_sospiri_%28Venice%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Bridge of Sighs in Venice, Italy","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAntonio_Contin_-_Ponte_dei_sospiri_(Venice).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia